  {
    "episode_id": 90171,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90171.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "exactly. Well, so we have patent protections for inventions in this country, including prescription drugs. And when those patent protections expire on a name brand drug, then that creates an opportunity for generic drug companies to come in and provide that medication for much, much less money. What's happening is that the name brand drug company says to the first generic drug company that wants to get in, hey, I'll promise not to sue you if you promise to just park that application that you have to bring your drug to market. And sometimes that goes on and on and on and on. And then other companies who might want to get in to the market and then proceed are kind of locked out because somebody else has already said, no, no, I'm going to make this drug. I'm just not doing it yet. And as I said, this sometimes goes on for years and years and years, and it's completely unfair. And I don't think that that is at all what should, that's not what we should be doing in this country. We should be making it easier for prescription generic medications that are just as safe and effective to get on the market and save people money.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Generic drugs are as safe and effective as their brand-name counterparts.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "Patent protections exist for inventions in this country, including prescription drugs.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "When patent protection for a name-brand drug expires, generic drug companies can enter the market.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Making generic prescription medications more accessible would save consumers money.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The described practice is considered unfair by the speaker.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Name-brand drug companies may offer not-to-sue agreements to first generic applicants in exchange for delaying market entry.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Such agreements can result in prolonged delays preventing other generic manufacturers from entering the market.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "These practices can last for years and create significant barriers to market access for generics.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Consumers have a right to affordable, safe, and timely access to essential medications.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The current patent system should promote competition and affordable access to medicines.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Delaying generic entry through non-litigation agreements undermines public interest in lower drug prices.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Generic drug approval processes are capable of being streamlined without compromising safety or efficacy.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Drug pricing transparency and fairness are important societal values.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Pharmaceutical companies act strategically rather than purely ethically in setting market behavior.",
        "confidence": 0.9
